@@ -76,6 +76,7 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA` / `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE` | **ON when a PCA companion GGUF is present, and there is nothing to turn on when there is not.** `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA=0` makes the loader skip the companion entirely; `GGML_ARIFI_SX8_PCA_FILE=<path>` names one instead of the auto-discovered `<model>.sx8pca.gguf` beside the model. Added 2026-09-15 (lane-242/R53). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` weights reached through `build_lora_mm`. S-X8 v4.3 stores two signed-4-bit PCA coefficients in byte 29 of every 30-byte block, but the PCA bases and scales live only in the `.sx8v43` container - the author's GGUF converter drops them and his llama.cpp patch ignores the coefficients. `tools/sx8/extract_sx8_pca.py` pulls the bases/scales out of the container over HTTP ranges and `tools/sx8/add_sx8_pca_to_gguf.py` writes them, with the unpacked coefficient planes, into a companion GGUF (366 MB for the 4B, against a 3.9 GB rewrite of the model file). The correction is built from stock ggml ops in `src/llama-graph.cpp` (`Z0[kb] = s0*sum_t X*b0`, `Y[n] += c0*Z0 + c1*Z1`), so CPU, Vulkan and any future backend get it from one place and no kernel or shader knows about PCA. **With no companion file the map is empty and the graph is the pre-R53 graph, bit for bit.** One receipt line at load names how many tensors were attached (that line is **in no captured receipt**; the "98 tensors" figure is a code prediction, not an observation). The companion carries an **identity stamp** (`sx8pca.source.name`/`.tensor`/`.head`) that the loader compares against the model's own bytes and refuses on mismatch, so a companion from a different quantization of the same-shaped model cannot attach silently -- **the guard is UNBUILT and UNRUN as of this row, and a companion written before it warns and loads**. | Leave it alone when a companion is beside the model. Use `=0` for the OFF arm of a quality pairing on the same binary and the same files. | **QUALITY: NO MEASURABLE PPL EFFECT; speed UNMEASURED.** `test-backend-ops MUL_MAT` is 2128/2128, 0 FAIL on **Vulkan0** in both arms -- the harness printed `Skipping CPU backend`, so the CPU `MUL_MAT` surface was never swept (its synthetic tensors carry no companion, so the arms are the same code either way). `test-sx8-pca` runs a real 64-row slice of `blk.0.ffn_down.weight` against float64 goldens taken straight from the spec: the PCA term lands within `7.3e-07` of the output on CPU and `4.3e-08` on Vulkan, and the two backends agree to `8.2e-07`. A planted coefficient-sign flip moves the term by `1.5x` and is caught. The pre-existing `2.159e-03` core gap against an exact decode is the Q8_1 activation quantization of the S-X8 mat-mul, identical on both backends and untouched by R53. PPL wikitext-2 on the 4B (`llama-perplexity -c 512`, 580 chunks, one cell at a time): S-X8 PCA OFF **9.9989** (+/-0.07217), PCA ON **9.9955** (+/-0.07213), Q8_0 **9.9742**. The ON/OFF delta is **-0.034%: sign only, indistinguishable from zero** -- 0.0034 PPL is ~5% of one cell's stderr, and two shorter receipts (4-chunk smoke, 8-chunk float-route probe) have the opposite sign. It must NOT be quoted as a measured gain; the gate for a signed claim is a paired per-chunk NLL dump. The id57 GGUF **is** the same quantization run as the container the bases came from (all six block fields byte-identical over 20.6M blocks on two tensors, `r53-evidence/40-verify-gguf-matches-container.txt`, EXIT=0), so this is a measurement of the real correction, not an artefact. The correction is oriented correctly -- it moves the weights TOWARD a Q8_0 reference on every tensor tested (-3.0% to -3.4% RMS error), while a sign flip and a c0/c1 swap both move them away -- but it does **NOT** reproduce the -2.64% of the author's Table 4. On this file the dropped PCA coefficients are worth nothing measurable in perplexity at 580 chunks. Absolute values are not comparable to his: he scores 512-token windows at stride 128, `llama-perplexity` does not. **Not applied to the embedding LOOKUP** (`token_embd` via `get_rows`), which is not a matmul - that arm of the correction is OWED. |
 | `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS` / `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_WG` | **unset = the probed default** (workgroup = the device subgroup width when it is a multiple of 32, else 32; rows = 2). Read once at pipeline creation. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a), extended 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: every S-X8 `mul_mat_vec` pipeline, f32/f16/q8_1/id alike. An override is taken WHOLE or refused WHOLE against this device's real limits: workgroup a positive multiple of 32, within `maxComputeWorkGroupInvocations`, and `NUM_COLS * rows * wg * 4` within `maxComputeSharedMemorySize` at the WIDEST `NUM_COLS`. **Setting either variable also switches the R46b shape-tuned lookup OFF for the whole run** — a run that kept the measured table on the cells the arm does not name would be a mixed arm. A stderr receipt names APPLIED or REFUSED once per distinct pair. | Sweep instrument. Use it to pair rows 1/2/4/8 against workgroups 64/128/256 out of one binary, or to pin the pre-R46b behaviour for an A/B. Not a serve setting. | On the 780M (subgroup 64, 32 KiB shared) `rows=8/wg=256` is REFUSED by shared memory and the run keeps the default; `rows=4/wg=256` and `rows=8/wg=128` sit exactly on the 32,768-byte limit, are admitted, and measured 12/12 GREEN on `MUL_MAT` (`research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/33-boundary-rows4-wg256.txt`, `34-boundary-rows8-wg128.txt`). |
 | S-X8 mat-vec shape tuning (R47d lookup) | **Code, not an environment variable. ON by device probe** — subgroup width 64 with `maxComputeSharedMemorySize == 32768`, the SUBGROUP workgroup class, f32 activations, and only for the exact (m, k, n) cells the table names. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit D). | Scope: `GGML_TYPE_SX8` `mul_mat_vec` with f32 activations. 31 measured cells over the four real 27B S-X8 decode shapes at n=1..8; the n=1 FFN orientations take rows=4/wg=256 and the 248320x5120 output takes rows=8/wg=64, which is why one global pair cannot express it. Every other shape, every n>8, the f16/q8_1/id paths and every other device keep the probed default. A stderr receipt at pipeline creation names ON/OFF and the probed default. | Nothing to operate. To measure against it, set `GGML_VK_SX8_MMV_ROWS`/`_WG` (which turns the lookup off whole). | **MEASURED on lane-232's base, which does NOT carry R46's `n <= 3` whole-block decode.** Every selected cell read >= 0.97 of the probed default in BOTH rounds, best cells +12.18% (248320x5120 n=2) and +10.76% (n=6); source `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-11-lane-232-r47a-alloc-instrument/R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Sweep selection". R46 replaces the decode math and row-blocking loop at `n <= 3` and leaves the reduction shape untouched, so the shared-memory arithmetic still holds, but **the n=1/2/3 optima are UNVERIFIED on the rebased kernel** until they are re-measured on the R46b stage. No held-out deployment guarantee is claimed. |
+| `GGML_VK_UMA_PLACEMENT` = `auto` \| `legacy` \| `device-local` | **`auto`** (unset = auto): device probe. AMD device id `0x150e` (Radeon 890M) gets `device-local`; every other device, the 780M included, gets `legacy`, the upstream UMA chain byte for byte. Added 2026-09-30 (x1-first-contact). A startup line names the chain in force, with tracing off. | Scope: the UMA branch of `ggml_vk_placement_attempts`, which is the chain for every device buffer (weights, KV, compute, prealloc scratch) and the one the batch planner walks. `legacy` = `{DL\|HV\|HC, DL, HV\|HC}`. `device-local` = `{DL, HV\|HC}`: plain `DEVICE_LOCAL` first, overflow to the host-visible heap, and `DL\|HV\|HC` left out so no byte is charged to the device-local heap while the driver bills the shared segment. Staging, pinned host memory and `GGML_VK_PREFER_HOST_MEMORY` are untouched. | Default on the 890M. Set `device-local` on another UMA device only after its own counters show the `DL\|HV\|HC` type billed to the Windows shared segment; set `legacy` to reproduce the pre-fix placement. | **CAUSE MEASURED 2026-09-30 on the X1 (890M, driver 26.8.1, 24 GB BIOS reservation).** Legacy: weights take memtype 2 (`DL\|HV\|HC`, heap 1). The driver reports heap-1 usage, but Windows bills the SHARED segment (1.4 to 11.2 GB, free RAM 14.4 to 4.1 GB, dedicated flat). Every load past ~11.2 GiB then dies at the first upload submit with `vk::Queue::submit: ErrorUnknown`. Memtype 0 (`DL`): dedicated 0.75 to 11.48 GB, shared flat, free RAM flat, the IQ3_S load that failed runs. Evidence: `research/local-inference/lane-evidence/2026-09-30-x1-first-contact/`. Device-vs-driver is not separated: the 780M was never run on driver 26.8.1. |
 | `GGML_VK_PLACEMENT=bulk-large-heap` | **OFF when unset; unset changes no byte of the existing placement chain.** Only the exact string `bulk-large-heap` is accepted. Added 2026-09-11 (lane-230/R46b commit E). | Scope: the bulk device buffers the model loader suballocates weights from (`ggml_backend_vk_buffer_type_alloc_buffer`), and nothing else — prealloc scratch and staging keep their current paths. The policy asks for a `DEVICE_LOCAL` type on the LARGEST heap and excludes every other heap for those buffers; grouping is untouched. A refusal is not fatal: that one buffer falls back to the stock chain and says so on stderr. A receipt names the chosen heap **with tracing off**. | Only to test the R47d placement hypothesis, in proof cells A/B, against an unchanged-policy control. | **UNMEASURED as a fix — commissioned, not proven.** R47d established the mechanism it targets: the placement chain tests each allocation against the WHOLE heap size with no cumulative accounting (`ggml-vulkan.cpp` `ggml_vk_find_memory_properties`), so `GGML_VK_PREFER_HOST_MEMORY=1` puts bulk weights on host-visible heap 1 and a 256 MiB grouping puts them in the small combined-property heap 2, and the first pinned upload then dies at `submit seq=0` with `ErrorUnknown` in both arms (`R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`, "Exact failure boundaries"). The driver-internal cause of `ErrorUnknown` is **UNSCORED**. The cumulative requirement reservation with rollback that the same commission asks for is **NOT implemented**; this is heap selection only. |
 | `GGML_VK_ALLOC_TRACE=1` | **OFF when unset, zero cost.** Added 2026-09-11 (lane-232/R47a). | Scope: diagnostic only. Prints requirement bytes, `memoryTypeBits`, every candidate memory type/heap tried, the chosen type and heap, per-heap live bytes, the driver's `VK_EXT_memory_budget` figures (or `budget=unavailable`, never fabricated zeros), and one line per queue submit with its copy batch and `VkResult`. | Load-failure and placement investigation. Never in a timed run: the trace is large (10.6 MB for one 27B load) and writes on the hot path. | Diagnostic instrument, no performance claim. It is what produced the two exact failure boundaries in `R47D-TRACES-SWEEP.md`. |
 | `GGML_VK_STAGING_RESERVE` | **unset = the device-probed default, which is `suballocation_block_size` (1 GiB unless `GGML_VK_SUBALLOCATION_BLOCK_SIZE` or the device's max allocation size lowers it).** Decimal bytes; `0` disables the reserve; an unparsable value keeps the default rather than becoming 0. Read at device creation, never compiled in. Added 2026-09-14 (lane-230/R46b B7c). | Scope: one reservation in the B7a per-device heap ledger, taken on the heap that `sync_staging` really allocates from (`HOST_VISIBLE\|HOST_COHERENT\|HOST_CACHED`, falling back to `HOST_VISIBLE\|HOST_COHERENT`), charged during `ggml_vk_get_device()` **before any bulk weight buffer can be planned or admitted**, and released exactly once in `~vk_device_struct`. It allocates nothing: it is a floor in the ledger that upload staging, prealloc scratch and compute buffers can still spend and weights cannot. The default is a real requirement, not a guess - a bulk weight buffer is capped at `suballocation_block_size`, one tensor lives inside one such buffer, and `sync_staging` is sized to one copy, so that block size is the largest single upload chunk this device can be asked to stage. | Leave unset. Lower it on a device whose host-visible heap is small enough that 1 GiB of floor refuses a load that used to fit; `0` restores pre-B7c admission exactly. | **Mechanism, not a performance claim: nothing here was timed and no model was loaded.** The reserve is charged before admission (`test-backend-ops heapres`, "the reserve is already charged before any bulk admission", probe reports 1073741824 B on heap 1 of 11311906816 B on the 780M) and releases exactly once (`heapres`, "a second release stole nothing"). **It reduces the bytes a load may take on that heap** - the R46b B7c report's positive HQ cell (27B Q4_K_XL still fits without a split) is what proves the default is safe on this rig, and it has not been run. |
